Cross-module mocking is UNVERIFIED — this test's generated test bodies are empty, so it was passing vacuously
=========================================================================

PARKED 2026-08-06, same root cause as 395_002_mock_multiple_branches. Read that
marker for the full mechanism and the open question; this file records only what
is specific to this test.

WHAT WAS MEASURED

`post.sh` runs `zig test output_emitted.zig` and reported "All 3 tests passed".
The blocks it passed, verbatim:

    test "Cross-module mock with failed branch" {
    }
    test "Cross-module mock should return 3 lines" {
    }

Empty, and the artifact contains ZERO `assert` calls — so neither
`assert(result.len > 0)` nor `assert(result == 3)` nor either `assert.fail()`
guard exists in the emitted program. The `test(...)` body is re-parsed under the
synthetic filename `__test__.kz`, where a Koru construct must open with `~`; this
is a `.k` with no prefixes, so the body parses as Zig host lines and nothing is
collected.

WHY THIS ONE MATTERS MORE THAN ITS SIBLING

The header claims a specific frontier was CLOSED: "Cross-module mocking × EFFECT
BRANCHES (FRONTIERS gap 5 — CLOSED 2026-06-12)", and describes a real fix —
`emitContinuationList` dropping effect (`!`) continuations so a result-dispatch
switch is over terminals only, where previously the mock path "leaked `.line =>`
against an Output union with no such tag and Zig rejected it".

That fix may well be real; 650_001 is cited as green end-to-end for the effect
path. But THIS test cannot be the evidence for it. Its emitted bodies are empty,
so the mock path it claims to exercise is never built, and the Zig rejection it
says it prevents could not occur either way. A closed-frontier claim resting on a
vacuous test is the thing to fix here, independently of which spelling turns out
to be correct — so when this is resolved, check whether gap 5 still has a
witness anywhere else, and if not, that frontier is open again.

Note the artifact does mention `read-lines` (5 occurrences) — the flow
`process-config` and its `std/fs:read-lines` call ARE emitted, because those sit
at file scope where `.k` parsing is fine. Only the `test(...)` BODIES are lost.
So this is narrowly a test-body dialect problem, not a module-resolution or
effect-lowering problem, and the surrounding program is not evidence that the
test ran.

ACCEPTANCE WHEN RESOLVED

The emitted `test` blocks must be non-empty, the artifact must contain this
file's four assertions, and the `failed`/`done` mocks must be visible in the
emitted mock substitution. Then delete this marker.
